<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <color name="translucentGreyBackground">#80696969</color>
    <color name="realTranslucent">#00000000</color>

    <!--
        Palettes of the two dark themes and of the light one.

        The screen colour is the theme's android:colorBackground (the window background of
        every screen) and the container colour is its colorSurface: the toolbar, the menus,
        the dialogs and - for the same reason - the cards the settings screens draw behind
        their rows. One pair per theme is what keeps those four surfaces in agreement, and
        every theme now declares both attributes. The light ones used to declare neither: the
        light screens and dialogs came out in Material3's near white (#FFFBFE) while the
        settings screens painted themselves with these values, so the app disagreed with itself
        and the owner read the near-white screen as glare.

        Light is the only family whose container is a little DARKER than its screen, and that
        is the stock relationship the owner asked for: a slightly grey screen, with menus,
        dialogs and cards a touch greyer and cooler on top of it so they read as surfaces
        instead of holes. The step is about 8% of luminance - visible, not glaring.

        Dark is a dark grey theme with clearly lighter containers; black is the AMOLED one,
        pure black with containers only as light as they have to be to stay visible.
    -->
    <color name="appScreenLight">#F6F6FA</color>
    <color name="appSurfaceLight">#F0EFF6</color>
    <color name="appScreenDark">#1E1F22</color>
    <color name="appSurfaceDark">#35383E</color>
    <color name="appScreenBlack">#000000</color>
    <color name="appSurfaceBlack">#22252A</color>

    <!-- Accent of the app: the fill of the action buttons and the label colour of the light
         dialogs' actions. Material Blue 700 matches the dark palettes, which have used it for
         years, and is close enough to the #1565C0 of the launcher and splash icons to read as
         the same blue. Without it the light default theme fell back to the colour Material3
         puts in colorAccent through its AppCompat bridge - ?attr/colorSecondary, a grey purple
         - which is the dirty purple the filled buttons came out in. -->
    <color name="appAccent">#1976D2</color>

    <!-- Foreground of everything Material paints with ?attr/colorPrimary in the dark themes, and
         of the app bar. Pure white, one value for all of it: the app bar title and its icons, the
         checked checkbox and its tick, the labels of dialog actions, the text of popup menus. The
         Material3 defaults are what made those either grey (colorOnSurfaceVariant, nv80) or dark
         on dark (colorPrimary #212121, colorOnPrimary #381E72 - a dark purple, which is the tick
         nobody could see). Seconds apart in value, visibly apart on screen: the title looked
         brighter than the icons next to it even when both came from the same near white. -->
    <color name="appOnDarkSurface">#FFFFFF</color>
</resources>
